==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*sitjaną}}, from *{{term||set-}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*sed-||sit}}.  Cognate with Old Frisian ''[[sitta]]'' (West Frisian {{term|sitte|lang=fy}}), Old High German ''[[sizzan]]'', {{term|sizzen|lang=goh}} (German ''[[sitzen]]''), Old Saxon ''[[sittian]]'', Dutch ''[[zitten]]'', Old Norse ''[[sitja]]'' (Icelandic ''[[sitja]]'', Swedish ''[[sitta]]''), Gothic {{term|sc=Goth|𐍃𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽|tr=sitan|lang=got}}.  The [[Proto-Indo-European]] root is also the source of Sanskrit {{term|sc=Deva|सीदति|tr=sīdati|lang=sa}}, Old Armenian {{term|նիստ||sitting|sc=Armn|tr=nist|lang=xcl}}, Old Irish ''[[saidid]]'' (Irish ''[[suigh]]''), Ancient Greek {{term|ἕζομαι|tr=hezomai|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}, Latin {{term|sedeō|lang=la}}, Old Church Slavonic {{term|sc=Cyrs|сѣдѣти|tr=sěděti|lang=cu}} (Russian {{term|сидеть|tr=sidét’|lang=ru|sc=Cyrl}}).

